        <!-- SECTION 1: How Weed Killers Actually Work -->
        <div id="how-weed-killers-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Weed Killers Actually Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every product in this guide falls into one of a small number of mechanisms, and knowing which one you are holding explains almost everything about how it will behave in your yard: how fast it works, whether the weed comes back, and what it will do to the plants around it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Systemic herbicides</strong> are absorbed through the leaves and travel through the plant vascular system down into the roots. This is why they are slow, often taking a week or more to show meaningful damage, and it is also why they are the only category that reliably kills perennial weeds outright. The plant is dying from the inside for days before it looks like anything is happening on the surface.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Contact herbicides</strong> destroy plant tissue wherever the spray physically lands. They are dramatically faster, often producing visible wilting within hours, but they never reach the root. On an annual weed with a shallow root system, that is often enough to finish the job. On a dandelion or a patch of bindweed, the crown survives and sends up new growth within a couple of weeks.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Pre-emergent herbicides</strong> do not kill living plants at all. They create a chemical barrier in the top inch of soil that prevents germinating seeds from establishing. Applied before the weeds appear, they are the most labor-efficient product category in home weed control, and the one most homeowners skip entirely because it feels like treating a problem that is not there yet.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Soil-residual herbicides</strong> combine a knockdown ingredient with a compound that stays active in the soil for months, preventing anything from re-establishing. They are extraordinarily effective in gravel and cracked pavement, and genuinely destructive if used anywhere near roots you care about.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
