        <!-- SECTION 2: Selective vs Non-Selective: The Decision That Matters Most -->
        <div id="selective-vs-non-selective" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Selective vs Non-Selective: The Decision That Matters Most
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the single distinction that separates a successful weekend from a lawn full of dead brown patches, and it is the one that product labels communicate least clearly.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Non-Selective Weed Killers</h3>

          <p>
            A non-selective herbicide kills essentially any plant it lands on. Glyphosate, diquat, and horticultural vinegar are all non-selective. They belong on driveways, gravel, patio cracks, fence lines, and areas being cleared for new landscaping. They do not belong on a lawn, because grass is a plant and the product does not know the difference. The most common weed killer failure we hear about is someone spraying a non-selective product across a weedy lawn and being genuinely surprised when the lawn dies alongside the weeds.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Selective Weed Killers</h3>

          <p>
            A selective herbicide exploits biochemical differences between plant families to kill some plants and spare others. The most common home category targets broadleaf weeds while leaving grasses unharmed, which is exactly what you want for dandelions, clover, and plantain in turf. Selectives are more specific and therefore more demanding of the buyer: a product that is safe on cool-season fescue may seriously injure a St. Augustine lawn, so matching the product to your grass type is not optional.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule is simple. If the weed is growing in something you want to keep alive, you need a selective. If the weed is growing in gravel, cracks, or bare ground, a non-selective is faster, cheaper, and more thorough. Buying one product to do both jobs is the mistake, not a shortcut.
          </p>

