        <!-- SECTION 4: Timing: Why the Calendar Beats the Product -->
        <div id="timing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Timing: Why the Calendar Beats the Product
          </h2>

          <p>
            Weed control is far more sensitive to timing than to which brand you buy. The same product applied in the right week can outperform a better product applied in the wrong one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Early Spring: The Pre-Emergent Window</h3>

          <p>
            Crabgrass and most annual weeds germinate when soil temperature at a two-inch depth holds around 55 degrees Fahrenheit for several consecutive days. A pre-emergent applied before that threshold prevents the entire season of crabgrass in one pass. Applied two weeks after it, the seeds have already sprouted and the barrier is useless against them. The traditional local cue is to apply when forsythia finishes blooming, which tracks soil temperature better than any calendar date.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Late Spring and Early Summer: Post-Emergent on Young Weeds</h3>

          <p>
            Young, actively growing weeds absorb herbicide far more efficiently than mature ones. A dandelion treated at the rosette stage takes a much lower dose than the same plant treated after it has flowered and set a deep taproot. If you find yourself repeatedly disappointed by weed killer results, spraying earlier in the weed life cycle usually fixes more than switching products does.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fall: The Most Underrated Window</h3>

          <p>
            Fall is the best time of year to treat perennial broadleaf weeds, and almost nobody does it. As temperatures drop, perennials move sugars downward into their root systems to store for winter, and a systemic herbicide applied in that window rides along with them straight to the roots. A fall application on dandelion and clover consistently produces better long-term control than an identical spring application.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">When Not to Spray</h3>

          <p>
            Skip application when temperatures are above the mid-80s, since heat-stressed plants slow their metabolism and absorb less. Skip windy days entirely, because drift damage to neighboring plants is the most common regret we hear. And check the rainfast window on the label before spraying ahead of rain, as most systemics need at least 30 minutes to a few hours of dry time to be absorbed.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Matching the Weed Killer to Where You Are Spraying -->
        <div id="matching-the-area" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Matching the Weed Killer to Where You Are Spraying
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Lawns and Turf</h3>

          <p>
            Use a selective broadleaf herbicide matched to your grass type, or a professional-grade turf product like Tenacity on cool-season grass. Never use glyphosate or vinegar on a lawn except to deliberately kill a patch you plan to reseed. Combine a spring pre-emergent with a targeted post-emergent spot treatment and you will do far less work than someone blanket-spraying all summer.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Driveways, Gravel, and Paver Cracks</h3>

          <p>
            This is where a soil-residual product earns its cost. There is nothing here you want to grow, so a year-long residual saves repeated trips with a sprayer. The only caution is drainage: if the gravel slopes toward a planting bed, use straight glyphosate instead and accept the repeat applications.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Garden and Landscape Beds</h3>

          <p>
            Pre-emergent granules are the primary tool, applied in early spring and again mid-season, with hand pulling or careful spot treatment for anything that gets through. If you spray anything non-selective in a bed, use a shield or a cardboard barrier to block drift, and spray on a completely still day.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fence Lines and Property Edges</h3>

          <p>
            These are the areas that get away from homeowners fastest because they are out of sight. A concentrate glyphosate in a backpack or pump sprayer is the efficient choice, and two applications a season, one in late spring and one in early fall, keeps most fence lines under control.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Near Vegetables and Edibles</h3>

          <p>
            Stick to horticultural vinegar, hand pulling, and mulch. Read every label carefully for pre-harvest intervals, and when in doubt about a product near food crops, do not use it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Application Technique That Actually Changes Results -->
        <div id="application" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Application Technique That Actually Changes Results
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two people spraying the same product on the same weeds can get very different outcomes. Most of the difference comes down to a handful of technique details that labels mention briefly and buyers skip.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Add a surfactant to bare concentrates.</strong> Many weeds have waxy leaf surfaces that cause spray droplets to bead up and roll off. A non-ionic surfactant breaks that surface tension so the herbicide stays in contact long enough to be absorbed. Premium formulas include one; budget concentrates usually do not, and adding your own is the single highest-impact upgrade to a cheap glyphosate jug.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wet the leaves, do not drench the ground.</strong> Systemic herbicides enter through foliage, not soil, so spraying until liquid runs off the leaves and puddles on the ground wastes product and increases drift and runoff risk. Aim for even coverage that leaves the leaf surface wet but not dripping.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Spray in the morning on a still day.</strong> Plants are actively transpiring in the morning, wind is typically lowest, and you avoid the midday heat that slows uptake. Anything above a light breeze is enough to carry fine droplets onto plants you did not intend to treat.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Give systemics the full waiting period.</strong> The most wasteful habit we see is re-spraying at day three because nothing looks dead. Glyphosate is already working at that point. Re-spraying doubles your cost and your chemical load for no additional effect.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">The Mistake That Wastes the Most Product</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Mowing right before or right after spraying a lawn. Mowing before removes the leaf surface the herbicide needs to be absorbed through, and mowing within a couple of days after removes treated foliage before the chemical has fully translocated to the roots. Wait two days on either side of a spray for meaningfully better results at zero extra cost.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Safety, Pets, and Rainfast Windows -->
        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Safety, Pets, and Rainfast Windows
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every product here is a pesticide, and the label is a legal document rather than a suggestion. The good news is that the practical safety rules are short and easy to follow.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Keep pets and children off treated areas until dry.</strong> This is the standard guidance on nearly every consumer herbicide label, and drying time is typically a few hours depending on temperature and humidity. Granular pre-emergents generally require watering in, after which the area is safe once the surface dries.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wear gloves and eye protection when mixing concentrates.</strong> The mixing step, when you are handling undiluted product, carries far more exposure risk than the spraying step. This applies especially to 20% horticultural vinegar, which is strong enough to cause chemical burns and serious eye injury despite reading as the natural choice on the shelf.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Respect the rainfast window.</strong> Rain falling before a systemic herbicide has been absorbed washes it off the leaf and onto the soil, wasting the application and putting the chemical where you did not want it. Rainfast times range from about 15 minutes for diquat to 30 minutes or more for glyphosate formulations, and longer for some selectives. Check the label and give yourself a margin.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store concentrates properly.</strong> Keep them sealed, out of freezing temperatures, and out of reach in an original labeled container. Never decant herbicide into a beverage bottle, which is a genuinely dangerous habit and a leading cause of accidental pesticide poisoning in homes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, mind your neighbors and any water features. Herbicide drift onto a neighbor garden is both a real harm and a real liability, and most consumer herbicides carry label restrictions on application near ponds, streams, and storm drains. A coarse spray setting, a still day, and a shield around the nozzle prevent nearly all of these problems.
          </p>
        </div>

