            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best weed killer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For general non-selective use on driveways, gravel, and fence lines, Roundup Weed & Grass Killer Super Concentrate offers the best combination of root-killing systemic action and cost per mixed gallon. For weeds growing in a cool-season lawn, Tenacity Turf Herbicide is the strongest selective option available to homeowners. For gravel areas where you want a full year of bare ground, Ortho GroundClear Year Long is the most labor-saving choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between selective and non-selective weed killer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A non-selective weed killer kills any plant it contacts, including lawn grass and ornamentals, and belongs on gravel, cracks, and fence lines. A selective weed killer targets specific plant families, most commonly killing broadleaf weeds while leaving grasses unharmed, which is what makes it safe to use across a lawn.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does weed killer take to work?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Contact herbicides such as diquat and horticultural vinegar show visible wilting within two to six hours and complete browning within a day. Systemic herbicides such as glyphosate take seven to fourteen days to fully kill a plant because they must travel from the leaves down into the root system, and re-spraying before that window is complete simply wastes product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is there a weed killer that is safe for lawns?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Selective broadleaf herbicides are formulated to kill weeds like dandelion, clover, and plantain without harming turf grass. Tenacity is the strongest option for cool-season lawns and can even be applied at seeding time, but it is not labeled for warm-season grasses like bermuda, zoysia, or St. Augustine, which need a product matched to that turf type.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When should I apply a pre-emergent weed killer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Apply pre-emergent when soil temperature at a two-inch depth reaches about 55 degrees Fahrenheit for several consecutive days, which is when crabgrass and most annual weeds begin germinating. A common regional cue is to apply as forsythia finishes blooming. Applied after germination has started, a pre-emergent will not control weeds that are already up.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will weed killer harm my pets?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most consumer herbicide labels direct you to keep pets and children off treated areas until the spray has fully dried, typically a few hours. Granular pre-emergents should be watered in and allowed to dry before pets return. Handling undiluted concentrate carries far more risk than the dried treated area, so gloves and eye protection at the mixing stage matter most.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Weed Killer Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every weed killer complaint we see traces back to one of four errors, and none of them are about buying the wrong brand.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Spraying Non-Selective Herbicide on a Weedy Lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This is the most common and most expensive mistake in home weed control. Glyphosate and vinegar do not distinguish between a dandelion and your fescue, and the result is a lawn full of dead brown patches that take a full season and a bag of seed to recover. If the weed is in turf you want to keep, you need a selective product, full stop.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Re-Spraying Systemics Too Soon</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Glyphosate takes seven to fourteen days to show its full effect on a perennial weed, and the plant looks completely healthy for the first several days while it is already dying. Re-spraying at day three doubles your cost and chemical load for no additional result. Mark the date you sprayed and do not judge the outcome until two weeks have passed.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a Soil-Residual Product Near Trees or Beds</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Year-long vegetation killers contain soil-active ingredients that move with water and are absorbed through roots. Spraying them along a fence line that runs under a mature tree canopy, or uphill of a flower bed, can kill plants many feet away from where you aimed. Reserve residuals for gravel and pavement that drains away from anything you value.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping Pre-Emergent and Fighting Weeds All Summer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The single biggest reduction in annual weeding effort comes from a product most homeowners never buy. One well-timed pre-emergent application in early spring prevents the crabgrass and annual weeds that otherwise consume a full season of spot spraying and hand pulling. Skipping it guarantees the harder, more expensive job later.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
